---
// The payment plan, read as a four-year commitment rather than as a
// percentage split.
//
// The subdomain's version is a checklist of what to ask a sales contact.
// This one asks the question a hub is placed to ask: a construction-linked
// plan ties money to progress, and this is one of the few Dubai projects
// where the buyer can read the progress themselves.
import VerticalLayout from "../../layouts/VerticalLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "@dst/ui/PageHero.astro";
import Callout from "@dst/ui/Callout.astro";
import Faq from "@dst/ui/Faq.astro";

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "What is the Palm Central payment plan?",
    a: "Nakheel has not published exact terms for the current phase. The sold-out first phase used a documented <strong>20 / 50 / 30</strong> structure: 20% at booking, 50% across construction milestones, 30% on handover. Secondary listings describe something similar for Phase 2; that is not the developer's own statement.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does the booking payment come to?",
    a: "On the Phase 1 structure and the current AED 2.7 million entry price, a 20% booking would be <strong>AED 540,000</strong>. On the retired AED 2.5 million it was AED 500,000. Confirm the percentage before budgeting from either.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does construction-linked actually mean here?",
    a: "That instalments fall due as stages complete rather than on calendar dates. Palm Jebel Ali is unusual in that Nakheel publishes its own progress figures, so a buyer can compare what is being invoiced against what the developer says is built.",
  },
  {
    q: "What happens if handover slips past September 2030?",
    a: "That is set by the sale and purchase agreement, not by a brochure, and it is the clause worth reading before the ones about finishes. Ask for it in writing before booking.",
  },
];
---
<VerticalLayout
  vertical="palm-central"
  current="/palm-central/payment-plan/"
  prose
  title="Paying for Palm Central over four years, in Dubai"
  description="Nakheel has not published Phase 2 terms for Palm Central in Dubai. What the sold-out phase committed a buyer to, what a 20% booking comes to at today's entry price, and the one clause to read first."
  image="/covers/pc-payment.jpg"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Payment plan"
    title="Paying for Palm Central over four years, in Dubai"
    image="/covers/pc-payment.jpg"
    imageAlt="Cut-paper illustration: a row of small flat squares spaced evenly along a long straight line, the first one larger than the rest."
    imageKind="generated"
  >
    <p class="lede">
      The gap between booking a Palm Central apartment and holding the keys
      is <strong>about four years</strong>. What fills it is a schedule
      Nakheel has not published for the current phase — so the honest
      starting point is the phase that is documented, and the arithmetic it
      implies at today's prices.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>The structure that is on the record</h2>
    <p>
      Phase 1, released October 2025 and now sold out, used a
      <strong>20 / 50 / 30</strong> plan: a fifth at booking, half spread
      across roughly seven to eight construction milestones, and the
      remaining <strong>30% on handover</strong>, then dated August 2029. At
      that phase's AED 2.5 million entry price the booking payment was
      <strong>AED 500,000</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Apply the same shape to the current AED 2.7 million entry price and
      booking becomes <strong>AED 540,000</strong>, with roughly
      <strong>AED 1.35 million</strong> falling across construction and
      <strong>AED 810,000</strong> at handover. Treat that as arithmetic on
      an assumption, not as a quotation: the assumption is the part Nakheel
      has not confirmed.
    </p>

    <h2>Why "construction-linked" is worth more here than usual</h2>
    <p>
      A construction-linked instalment is normally something a buyer takes on
      trust: the invoice says a stage is complete and there is no independent
      way to check. Palm Jebel Ali is an exception, because Nakheel publishes
      progress for the island — <strong>26.75%</strong> overall, with
      building work at <strong>21.64%</strong> and MEP at
      <strong>18.76%</strong> — and a regulator's inspection sits alongside
      it: RERA's report of <strong>27 January 2026</strong> put the six
      fronds between <strong>15.60%</strong> and <strong>31.45%</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two published figures for the same thing, from the developer and from
      the regulator, is the most useful thing a buyer on a construction-linked
      plan can have. It will not settle a dispute, but it means the question
      "is this stage really done" has somewhere to start other than the
      invoice.
    </p>

    <h2>The four years, laid flat</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>At booking</strong> — the largest single payment, and the one made before any of the flat exists.</li>
      <li><strong>Through construction</strong> — the longest stretch, and the one where a delay moves money rather than cancelling it.</li>
      <li><strong>At handover, September 2030</strong> — the balance, due on a date four years away, which is long enough for a mortgage market and a personal situation to be unrecognisable.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Callout kind="warn">
    <p>
      Several broker sites publish exact Phase 2 percentages. They may well be
      right, but they are not Nakheel saying so, and an instalment schedule is
      not a detail to take from a listing. Ask for the schedule with dates
      attached, in writing, before the booking payment moves.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Next</h2>
    <p>
      The prices these percentages apply to are on
      <a href="/palm-central/prices/" title="Palm Central prices in Dubai, by unit type">the prices page</a>;
      the progress figures behind the milestones are on
      <a href="/palm-central/" title="Palm Central on Palm Jebel Ali, Dubai">the section front</a>;
      and if the purchase is also a residency plan, that is
      <a href="/palm-central/golden-visa/" title="Palm Central and the Dubai Golden Visa threshold">the Golden Visa page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqItems} />

  <section class="container">
    <p class="source-note">
      Phase 1's 20 / 50 / 30 structure, entry prices and handover dates:
      Nakheel's releases as carried on
      <a href="https://palmcentral.dst.llc/payment-plan/" title="The Palm Central payment plan">palmcentral.dst.llc</a>.
      Island progress percentages and the RERA inspection of 27 January 2026:
      Nakheel, construction progress for Palm Jebel Ali, read 22 September
      2026. Dirham figures at the current entry price are arithmetic on the
      Phase 1 structure and are not a developer quotation. Nothing here is
      financial advice.
    </p>
  </section>
</VerticalLayout>
